<!-- The template language, one page per SPELLING.

     The second of the two vocabularies `/docs` is keyed by, and the reason it exists is on the index
     above this one: a reader who has met `{#for}` or `bind:checked` in a `.abide` file has no callable
     to look up, because those import nothing. Every one of them used to be reachable only through
     `/tests/template`, which is a page about whether the language WORKS rather than what it says.

     Prose only, like `/docs` itself — `SPELLINGS.ts` carries no ladder — so listing all fifteen costs
     nothing that listing one would not.

     WHAT MAKES THIS LIST HONEST is that the compiler owns the closed half of it. The five blocks and
     the five `bind:` targets are enumerable, and `test/docs.test.ts` compares this list against
     `BRANCHES` and `BINDABLE` in both directions — so a sixth block is a red gate until it has a rung.
     The open half (`class:`, `style:`, a spread, an `on<event>`) takes any name there is and can only
     be gated on having a rung at all, which the same file checks. -->
<script module>
import { SPELLING_ORDER, SPELLINGS } from '../../../demos/SPELLINGS.ts'

const CARD = 'card'
</script>

<div class="page-head">
    <p class="crumb"><a href="/docs">docs</a></p>
    <h1 class="page-title">syntax</h1>
    <p class="page-blurb">Everything a <code>.abide</code> file says that is not a name you imported. Each page is that spelling’s ladder: every rung is the one before it plus exactly one new thing, and every rung is a real file this app compiles, renders and tests.</p>
</div>

<div class="name-grid">
    {#for slug of SPELLING_ORDER}
        <a href="/docs/syntax/{slug}" class={CARD} data-spelling={slug}>
            <code class="name">{SPELLINGS[slug].name}</code>
            <p class="card-blurb">{SPELLINGS[slug].blurb}</p>
        </a>
    {/for}
</div>

<p class="note">What a cell READ inside one of these means is the other vocabulary’s — see <a href="/docs/state" class="link">state</a>. What the language costs to compile is measured on <a href="/bench/template" class="link">/bench/template</a>.</p>
